# skysheet 1
#
# Every project is a constellation. Every skill is a star. A star that more
# than one project claims is drawn once and belongs to both — the geometry is
# the provenance.
#
#   constellation <id>
#     name   display name
#     year   when
#     repo   url, or "private"
#     at     x y   sky units, y grows downward. Keep neighbours at least a
#                  ring apart (see REST_X/REST_Y in layout.rs) or two projects
#                  overlap. Move these to rearrange the sky; nothing else depends
#                  on them. `skysheet --layout` shows what they produced.
#     blurb  the one-line version, shown large
#     about  the paragraph, shown when the project is opened
#     stats  languages, size, and whatever else is countable
#
#   star <id>
#     name   display name
#     in     constellation, ...   the FIRST one is where the story happened;
#                                 a * marks a project that leans on this skill
#     story  indented block. Two spaces starts a key, four continues a value.
#
# The stories are DRAFTS — inferred from topics.md and termap's README, in the
# voice those documents already use. They are the part to rewrite. Roughly half
# restate something those documents actually say (nftables' docker socket,
# watch-party's certainty gate, every termap entry); the rest are plausible
# reconstructions of how the skill was probably acquired, and are guesses.
#
# Dates: the years below come from `git log` in the repositories that are on
# this machine — netjail (Jul 2026), gitswitch (Apr 2026), watch-party (Jun–Aug
# 2026, 471 commits), logify (Mar–Jun 2026), termap (Aug 2026). clip, Noter,
# vcs and stylized-maps are not checked out here, so their years are guesses.

# ── constellations ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

constellation termap
  name   termap
  year   Aug 2026
  repo   local
  at     0 -115
  blurb  A map you pan around inside a terminal.
  about
    Every cell is a 2×4 braille grid, so an ordinary 160×46 terminal is really a
    320×184 framebuffer — enough to draw a coastline that looks like a
    coastline. It reads a PMTiles basemap, so the map can be a whole country at
    street level without that country having to fit in memory, and tilts into
    relief and then perspective as you zoom in.
  stats  Rust · 5,200 LOC · ratatui, crossterm, flate2 and nothing else · 1.6 GB archive → 19 MB resident

constellation stylized-maps
  name   stylized-maps
  year   2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/stylized-maps
  at     150 -65
  blurb  A custom vector-tile pipeline for stylized maps of India.
  about
    An end-to-end map asset pipeline: OSM extracts through Planetiler with custom
    Java profiles into vector tiles against a versioned normalized schema,
    served by a TypeScript API with an OpenAPI spec, rendered by a web client
    with hand-built cartographic styling. Ships with a resource-governed build
    CLI, because the full build is too heavy to run unattended.
  stats  Python + TypeScript + Java · in progress · Docker Compose behind Caddy

constellation watch-party
  name   watch-party
  year   Jun–Aug 2026
  repo   private
  at     190 25
  blurb  Self-hosted synchronized movie nights, frame-accurate across devices.
  about
    A watch-party platform over a Jellyfin media library: a web client and a
    native Flutter desktop app stay in sync to within ~120 ms p95 while playing
    the same title, with LiveKit voice chat and a Servarr integration for
    pulling in titles the library does not have yet. The interesting part is the
    sync engine — one pure decision core mirrored in JavaScript and Dart, held
    equivalent by generated conformance vectors run in both CIs.
  stats  Dart + TypeScript · ~115k LOC · 471 commits · 152 test files · 11 scenario suites

constellation logify
  name   logify
  year   Mar–Jun 2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/logify
  at     110 100
  blurb  Live container logs from a Coolify host, in your terminal.
  about
    Two halves of one tool. coolify_logs is the gateway that runs on the Docker
    host: a FastAPI service exposing REST and WebSocket endpoints for live
    container logs, with an admin UI for issuing API keys and scoping which
    containers each key can see. logify is the client — a Go TUI that browses
    projects and streams logs, shipped as a single static binary with one-line
    installers for macOS, Linux and Windows.
  stats  Go + Python · 4 tagged releases · CI · Docker Compose · proxy docs for Coolify, Nginx, Traefik

constellation noter
  name   Noter
  year   2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/Noter
  at     5 130
  blurb  A terminal notepad that keeps its own history.
  about
    A quick-access note-taking TUI built on Python curses and DuckDB, using your
    own $EDITOR for the actual editing rather than reimplementing one. Every
    change is committed to git behind the scenes, so notes have a real dated
    version history you can browse from inside the app, and it fires native
    system notifications.
  stats  Python · curses + DuckDB · git-backed versioning

constellation vcs
  name   vcs
  year   2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/vcs
  at     -105 115
  blurb  Git, rebuilt from scratch, to find out how it actually works.
  about
    A working toy version-control system implementing the real plumbing: init,
    commit, log, checkout, merge, reset and cat-file. Content-addressed object
    store, no network, no libraries doing the hard part. Written after reading
    the Git internals docs, as a way to stop treating git as a black box.
  stats  Python · pip-installable · deliberately not for production

constellation gitswitch
  name   gitswitch
  year   Apr 2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/gitswitch
  at     -185 50
  blurb  Stop committing with your work email on personal projects.
  about
    Manages multiple git identities and their SSH keys. Profiles live as TOML in
    ~/.config/git_conf/; pick one interactively and it rewrites the repository's
    local .git/config and sets core.sshCommand so the right key is used per
    repo. Solves the specific problem of two GitHub accounts on one machine.
  stats  Go · v1.0.0 tagged · interactive TUI forms

constellation clip
  name   clip
  year   2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/clip
  at     -190 -30
  blurb  Copy on one machine, paste on another — over your own tailnet.
  about
    A targeted cross-platform clipboard for a personal Tailscale fleet. Copy on
    your laptop and it lands on a selected machine's clipboard: text, images or
    files up to 20 MB. Each machine runs a daemon bound only to the Tailscale
    interface, never 0.0.0.0, so Tailscale's encryption and ACLs are the trust
    boundary and there is no application-level auth to get wrong.
  stats  Go, stdlib only, no cgo · single static binary · spec, plan and design docs

constellation netjail
  name   netjail
  year   Jul–Aug 2026
  repo   github.com/Prince2412k2/netjail
  at     -105 -95
  blurb  Run any command with only its network sandboxed.
  about
    A Linux sandbox that isolates what a process can reach on the network while
    leaving everything else untouched — same filesystem, same $HOME, same
    binaries, same user. No container, no VM, no volume mounts, no copied files.
    Enforcement is a real network namespace with a veth pair and nftables
    default-drop, plus a local filtering proxy that can allow or refuse
    individual HTTPS paths, not just whole hosts.
  stats  Go · 16,700 LOC · 28 packages · 6,100 lines of tests (37%) · threat model · 16k-word manual

# ── netjail ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star netns
  name   network namespaces
  in     netjail*, clip
  story
    The appeal of a namespace is what it does *not* touch. A container gives you
    isolation by replacing the world — filesystem, mounts, user, binaries — and
    then you spend the afternoon mounting your own home directory back in. A
    network namespace isolates one axis and leaves the other twenty alone, so
    the sandboxed process is still your process, in your shell, with your files.
    Everything else in netjail exists to close the holes that leaves open.

star veth
  name   veth pairs
  in     netjail*
  story
    A namespace with no interface is not a sandbox, it is an outage. The veth
    pair is the deliberate hole: one end inside, one end on the host, and every
    packet the process will ever send has to cross it. That is the whole reason
    the design is enforceable — there is exactly one wire to police, so a rule
    on it cannot be routed around.

star nftables
  name   nftables
  in     netjail*
  story
    Started with per-host allow rules, which is the wrong shape: anything nobody
    thought of yet is permitted. Default-drop and punch holes is the only
    ordering that fails closed, and it immediately found what the allow-list
    never would have — the host's own daemon sockets, reachable from inside the
    namespace and bypassing the wire entirely. A sandbox that fails open is not
    a sandbox.

star dns-proxy
  name   DNS proxying
  in     netjail*
  story
    Blocking addresses does nothing if the process can still ask for them. DNS
    is the seam where a policy written about names meets an enforcement layer
    that only understands addresses, and the resolver has to sit inside the
    sandbox or the two disagree — the process resolves one thing and the rules
    are about another.

star tls-ca
  name   CA injection
  in     netjail*
  story
    Filtering HTTPS means terminating it, and terminating it means the client
    has to trust you. Injecting a CA into the system trust path is the part
    where the sandbox stops being purely defensive and starts holding something
    genuinely dangerous, which is why the threat model had to be written down
    before the code was.

star http-filter
  name   path-level filtering
  in     netjail*
  story
    Host-level rules are too coarse to say anything useful. "Allow api.example.com"
    grants the read endpoint and the delete endpoint in the same breath. A local
    proxy that can allow or refuse an individual path is the difference between
    a firewall and a policy — and it is only reachable once the namespace has
    forced every connection through one place to be inspected.

star threat-model
  name   threat modelling
  in     netjail*, clip
  story
    Writing the threat model first is what turned a pile of rules into a design.
    It forces the question the code never asks on its own: what is this assuming,
    and what happens when that assumption is false. Half the bypasses in netjail
    were found in the document, before there was anything to bypass.

star systemd
  name   systemd units
  in     netjail*, logify
  story
    A tool that needs three commands and a privileged setup step before it works
    is a tool nobody runs twice. Units and an installer are not packaging chores;
    they are the difference between something that demos and something that is
    still on the machine a month later.

# ── watch-party ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star clock-sync
  name   clock synchronization
  in     watch-party*
  story
    Two players agreeing on a timestamp is easy. Two players agreeing on *now*,
    across a network that lies to you at random, is the whole problem. Raw offset
    samples are unusable — a single scheduler hiccup produces an outlier that a
    naive mean will chase for a minute. Rejecting outliers, weighting what
    survives by its own uncertainty, and carrying a skew term is the minimum that
    holds ~120 ms across devices.

star theil-sen
  name   Theil–Sen estimation
  in     watch-party*
  story
    Least squares fits the noise as eagerly as it fits the signal, and clock
    offset samples are mostly noise with occasional catastrophic spikes. Theil–Sen
    takes the median of pairwise slopes, so a bad sample gets one vote instead of
    a proportional one. It is slower and it does not care.

star pi-control
  name   PI control
  in     watch-party*
  story
    A player that snaps to the right position stutters; one that eases toward it
    drifts. A PI controller on playback rate does neither — but only if the error
    it is fed is gated on certainty. Left ungated it chases measurement noise it
    has no ability to observe, and the rate flutters audibly. The gate is the
    part that made it work.

star conformance
  name   conformance vectors
  in     watch-party*
  story
    The decision core is mirrored in JavaScript and Dart, which means there are
    two of it, which means they will diverge. Generated JSON vectors run in both
    CIs turn "these should behave the same" into something that fails a build
    instead of failing a movie night three weeks later.

star pure-core
  name   pure decision cores
  in     watch-party*
  story
    Sync logic tangled into a player is untestable — you cannot ask it what it
    would do without an actual video, an actual network and an actual clock.
    Pulling every decision into a pure function of state means the hard part can
    be tested exhaustively and offline, and the platform code shrinks to plumbing.

star hls
  name   adaptive streaming
  in     watch-party*
  story
    A seek is not free and pretending otherwise produces a chase loop: seek to
    where the other client is, arrive late because the seek cost time, seek
    again. Modelling the cost and aiming *ahead* to a rendezvous point is the fix
    — you do not seek to where they are, you seek to where you will both be.

star webrtc
  name   WebRTC / LiveKit
  in     watch-party*
  story
    Voice alongside video is a second real-time system with its own clock, its
    own jitter buffer and its own opinions about latency, running next to the one
    being carefully synchronized. Keeping them from fighting each other was more
    of the work than getting either to run.

star slo-testing
  name   SLO-driven testing
  in     watch-party*, netjail
  story
    "Feels in sync" is not a test. Stating drift and rate-flutter as numeric
    targets, then asserting them in a scenario harness, is what makes a
    regression visible on a branch rather than in a living room. Eleven suites
    exist because eleven different things turned out to break it.

star flutter
  name   Flutter / Dart
  in     watch-party*
  story
    Picked for one native desktop binary across macOS, Windows and Linux, which
    it delivers. The cost is that the sync core then has to exist in Dart as well
    as JavaScript — the conformance vectors are the bill for that choice.

# ── logify + coolify_logs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star fastapi
  name   FastAPI
  in     logify*
  story
    The gateway is mostly a careful boundary with a small amount of Python behind
    it. FastAPI's value is that the boundary is declared once and the schema, the
    validation and the docs all fall out of the same declaration, which matters
    when the client is a separate binary written in a different language.

star websockets
  name   WebSockets
  in     logify*
  story
    Logs are a stream, and polling a stream is a way of being both late and
    wasteful. The interesting part is not opening the socket, it is what happens
    when the container dies, the client is on a train, or the buffer fills faster
    than the reader drains it.

star docker-api
  name   Docker API
  in     logify*
  story
    Talking to the daemon directly instead of shelling out to `docker logs` is
    what makes the gateway able to scope what a key can see. It is also the same
    socket netjail spends effort blocking — access to it is access to the host,
    which is exactly why the key scoping had to come first.

star api-authz
  name   scoped API keys
  in     logify*
  story
    A key that can read every container is a key that can read your database
    credentials scrolling past in a log line. Scoping is per-key and per-container
    for that reason, and the admin UI exists so revoking one is a thing you
    actually do rather than a thing you mean to do.

star reverse-proxy
  name   reverse proxies
  in     logify*, stylized-maps
  story
    WebSockets are where reverse-proxy defaults go wrong quietly — buffering,
    timeouts, and upgrade headers each break the stream in a different way, and
    none of them log anything useful. Coolify, Nginx and Traefik each need
    different words for the same three fixes, so all three ended up documented.

star shipping
  name   shipping binaries
  in     logify*, clip, gitswitch, netjail
  story
    A single static binary and a one-line installer is a deliberate constraint,
    not a convenience: it means no runtime to match, no dependency to resolve,
    and nothing to explain in a README before someone can try the thing. Tagged
    releases and CI exist so that stays true on three operating systems.

# ── clip ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star tailnet
  name   Tailscale as trust boundary
  in     clip*
  story
    The strong version of this design is the restraint: bind only to the
    Tailscale interface, never to 0.0.0.0, and add no application-level auth at
    all. Tailscale's encryption and ACLs already are the boundary, and a second
    home-made one alongside it would be the weaker of the two. The whole security
    argument is one bind address.

star clipboard
  name   cross-platform clipboard
  in     clip*
  story
    Three operating systems with three unrelated ideas about what a clipboard is,
    and the usual answer is cgo. Doing it in pure Go, stdlib only, keeps the
    single static binary intact — which was the point of the project, so paying
    for it here would have undone it everywhere.

star invariants
  name   stated invariants
  in     clip*, watch-party
  story
    Two properties written down before the code: content received and written is
    never echoed back, and identical repeats are deduped by content hash. Both
    are one sentence, both are unit-tested, and the first one is the difference
    between a clipboard and an A→B→A loop that fills every machine on the tailnet.

# ── Noter ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star curses
  name   curses
  in     noter*
  story
    Under everything a TUI framework does is a screen you have to repaint
    yourself, and curses is that screen with nothing in front of it. Worth
    writing one app against directly — every later framework choice is easier to
    judge once you know what it is choosing for you.

star duckdb
  name   DuckDB
  in     noter*
  story
    Notes are small; queries over notes are not. An embedded columnar engine
    means no server, no schema migration ceremony, and a single file that moves
    with the app — the same trade as a static binary, one layer down.

star editor-handoff
  name   $EDITOR handoff
  in     noter*
  story
    The strongest decision in Noter is a thing it does not do. Reimplementing an
    editor is months of work to arrive somewhere worse than the one already
    configured. Shelling out to $EDITOR means the app inherits every keybinding,
    plugin and habit its user already has, for free.

star notifications
  name   desktop notifications
  in     noter*
  story
    Native notifications are the seam where a terminal app stops being confined
    to its terminal. Small, but it is the difference between a tool you have to
    remember to open and one that reaches you.

# ── vcs ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star content-address
  name   content-addressed storage
  in     vcs*
  story
    Writing the object store is what makes git stop being magic. Once the name of
    a thing *is* its hash, deduplication, integrity and immutability are not
    features anyone implemented — they are consequences, and you can see exactly
    where they come from.

star three-way-merge
  name   three-way merge
  in     vcs*
  story
    Merge is the one command that cannot be understood by reading its output.
    Implementing it — find the common ancestor, diff both sides against it, and
    admit when the two disagree — is what turned conflict markers from an
    annoyance into an obviously correct answer to an ambiguous question.

star git-internals
  name   git internals
  in     vcs*, gitswitch, noter
  story
    Read the internals docs, then rebuilt the plumbing — init, commit, log,
    checkout, merge, reset, cat-file — because reading them is not the same as
    knowing them. Everything downstream got easier: gitswitch edits config with a
    clear idea of what it is editing, and Noter's version history is a real
    commit graph rather than a table pretending to be one.

# ── gitswitch ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star git-config
  name   git config internals
  in     gitswitch*
  story
    The bug this fixes is a social one — a work email on a personal commit,
    discovered publicly and permanently. The fix is per-repo local config plus
    core.sshCommand, which is the only place the identity and the key can be set
    together and stay set. Global config cannot express "this repo, this person".

star ssh-keys
  name   SSH key management
  in     gitswitch*
  story
    Two GitHub accounts on one machine is a problem the tooling refuses to have
    an opinion about. Keys per profile, selected with the identity rather than
    alongside it, so the wrong key and the wrong email cannot come apart.

star tui-forms
  name   interactive forms
  in     gitswitch*, logify, noter
  story
    A config tool that makes you learn its flags before it can save you time has
    not saved you time. Interactive forms mean the discoverable path and the
    correct path are the same path, which for a tool used once a month is the
    only thing that matters.

# ── stylized-maps ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star planetiler
  name   Planetiler profiles
  in     stylized-maps*
  story
    The default profile decides what a map is allowed to contain, and its
    decisions are not yours — it drops admin boundaries and stops places at city
    level. Writing a custom Java profile is how you stop arguing with a pipeline
    and start specifying one.

star schema-versioning
  name   versioned tile schemas
  in     stylized-maps*
  story
    Tiles are a build artifact and a client contract at the same time. Without a
    version on the normalized schema, a re-run of the pipeline silently changes
    what the renderer is reading, and the bug shows up as cartography rather than
    as a diff.

star openapi
  name   OpenAPI
  in     stylized-maps*
  story
    A tile API is small enough to be tempting to leave undocumented, and it has
    exactly the kind of surface — coordinate order, zoom bounds, error shapes —
    where undocumented means wrong. The spec is the cheapest place to be precise.

star build-orchestration
  name   resource-governed builds
  in     stylized-maps*
  story
    The full build does not fit in the machine's spare capacity, so it needs to
    be interruptible: start, pause, resume, with auto/full/manual CPU and RAM
    profiles. A build you cannot pause is a build you run overnight and hope
    about, and hoping does not resume from where it failed.

star cartography
  name   cartographic styling
  in     stylized-maps*, termap*
  story
    A map is not a chart of everything present; it is a set of decisions about
    what to leave out. Both projects arrive at the same place from opposite
    ends — the web renderer by hand-building styles, termap by discovering that a
    20 km view drawing all 5,982 secondary roads is mush no shading can rescue.

# ── termap ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star rust
  name   Rust
  in     termap*
  story
    Chosen for a renderer that has to do real per-frame work inside a terminal,
    and earned mostly in the parts nobody planned: the buffers, the borrow
    discipline around a canvas that everything wants to write to, and getting a
    5,000-line program to keep a whole country at street level in 19 MB.

star subpixel
  name   subpixel rendering
  in     termap*
  story
    A braille cell is 2×4 dots, so a 160×46 terminal is really a 320×184
    framebuffer. Storing a float *coverage* and a *depth* per subpixel instead of
    an on/off bit is what buys two axes out of one monochrome cell: coverage
    decides which dots light, depth decides how bright. A feature can then be
    faint because it is thin, or faint because it is far — and those read
    differently.

star dithering
  name   ordered dithering
  in     termap*
  story
    A terminal has no fill shades to spare, so the sea and the parks go through
    an 8×8 ordered matrix and thin to a stipple. 8×8 rather than 4×4 because a
    4×4 repeats every two cells and the eye picks it up as corduroy. The other
    lesson was scale: density is per subpixel but legibility is per *cell*, and
    at 8% of subpixels roughly 64% of cells catch a dot and the texture reads as
    static.

star zbuffer
  name   z-buffering
  in     termap*
  story
    The canvas stored a depth per subpixel for a long time without ever *testing*
    it, so nothing was ever hidden. Turning the test on is not unconditional:
    in 2D, depth is a styling device — importance as distance — and the paint
    order is not monotonic in it, so testing there drops minor roads wherever
    they cross water. It is on only when the camera is tilted.

star projection
  name   oblique & perspective projection
  in     termap*
  story
    Oblique first, deliberately: rotate by bearing, compress y by cos(tilt), lift
    by sin(tilt). No vanishing point, no divide, and at tilt 0 it reduces exactly
    to the 2D path, so there is one code path rather than two. Perspective came
    later and only above z14, because convergence is the one cue a parallel
    projection cannot produce — and because anything that has passed behind the
    eye must be *rejected*, not clamped. Clamping flings it across the frame in a
    radial fan that looks precisely like a clipping bug.

star web-mercator
  name   Web Mercator
  in     termap*, stylized-maps
  story
    Converting lon/lat costs a ln and a sin, so features are converted once at
    load and never again — everything downstream works in world coordinates
    normalised to 0..1. Matching the slippy-map convention for tile size means
    zoom numbers mean the same thing here as in every other map tool, which is
    worth more than any cleverness would have been.

star pmtiles
  name   PMTiles
  in     termap*
  story
    Loading features eagerly costs ~220 bytes each and caps out around 2–3M —
    not enough for a country at street level. Streaming tiles removes the ceiling
    entirely: a 1.6 GB archive of India renders in 19 MB resident. The published
    crates are async-first and would have dragged a runtime into a synchronous
    program, so the directory format is parsed by hand. It is about 200 lines.

star mvt
  name   vector tiles
  in     termap*, stylized-maps*
  story
    Two lessons, both expensive. Exterior rings are clockwise in *tile* space,
    and tile space has y increasing downward — so clockwise is positive shoelace
    area, the opposite of the usual convention; get it backwards and you keep
    every hole and drop every polygon. And archives disagree with their own
    documentation about layer names, which is why there is a probe script: the
    schema tables were derived from what the tiles actually contain.

star dem
  name   heightmaps & hillshading
  in     termap*
  story
    Two decisions mattered more than they looked. Shade by *slope*, not
    elevation — drawn by height, terrain fills a whole city with texture and
    buries the map. And exaggerate relative to the ground under the view, not sea
    level: at 14×, a town on a 300 m plateau lifts by more than half the frame
    and slides the map off the top of the screen.

star label-placement
  name   label placement
  in     termap*
  story
    Greedy and rank-ordered: important names get first pick, and anything that
    cannot find a clear slot is pushed out on a leader line or dropped. Dropping
    is the feature. A map that refuses to draw an overlapping label is more
    readable than one that draws everything, and every attempt to be more
    generous made it worse.

star binary-formats
  name   hand-parsed binary formats
  in     termap*, vcs
  story
    Shapefile is a documented binary format and .dbf is dBase III, which together
    are far less trouble than a GDAL dependency. The trap is not the format, it
    is the detail nobody writes down: dBase pads with NUL rather than spaces, so
    a plain strip() leaves every name unusable and every label silently vanishes.

# ── the shared spine ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

star go
  name   Go
  in     netjail*, clip*, logify*, gitswitch*
  story
    Four projects, and the reason is the same each time: one static binary, no
    runtime to install, and a concurrency model that survives a TUI streaming
    logs while the network drops. netjail is where it got hard — 28 packages that
    all have to agree on the lifetime of one namespace.

star python
  name   Python
  in     noter*, vcs*, logify, stylized-maps
  story
    Where the work is exploratory or the shape is not known yet. Both times it
    was used for something durable — Noter and vcs — the honest description is
    that the point was understanding, not throughput, and Python got out of the
    way of that faster than anything else would have.

star typescript
  name   TypeScript
  in     stylized-maps*, watch-party*
  story
    Two uses that could not be less alike: a tile API with an OpenAPI spec, and
    half of a decision core whose other half is Dart and must stay behaviourally
    identical to it. The second one is where the type system stopped being a
    convenience and started being the thing holding the invariant.

star tui
  name   terminal UI design
  in     termap*, logify*, noter*, gitswitch
  story
    Four apps in three languages, and the recurring lesson is that a terminal's
    constraints are the design. There is one glyph per cell and no fill shades,
    so colour has to carry *kind* while brightness carries depth; there is no
    hover state to lean on, so anything discoverable has to be visible. Working
    within that is what makes the output look intentional rather than limited.

star docker
  name   Docker & Compose
  in     logify*, stylized-maps, netjail
  story
    Used for deployment in two projects and treated as an adversary in a third —
    the daemon socket is the bypass netjail spends real effort blocking. Both
    views are correct, and holding them at the same time is most of what
    understanding it means.

star tech-writing
  name   technical writing
  in     netjail*, termap*, clip
  story
    A design doc with a threat model, a 16,000-word manual, and READMEs that
    argue for their decisions rather than list their features. The writing is not
    documentation of the work — several times it *was* the work, because a
    bypass, a wrong ordering or a bad abstraction is much cheaper to find in a
    paragraph than in a program.

star testing
  name   testing discipline
  in     netjail*, watch-party*, clip
  story
    6,100 lines of tests against 16,700 of code in one project; 152 test files
    and an eleven-suite scenario harness in another. The shape that made them
    worth writing was pulling the decisions into something pure and testable
    first — after that the tests are cheap, and before it they are theatre.
